// FlashAttention tile shape, for datagen.py.
//
// SHAPE 1 only: S^T = K.Q^T. The geometry the kernel sees follows from these and the mesh
// in the cluster cfg, and datagen.py emits it into data.h:
//
//   Bc = M*meshRow    key columns  -- the tiling knob, grow it until TCDM is full
//   Br = N*meshCol    query rows
//   d  = K*tileSize   head dimension -- a property of the MODEL, not a knob
//
// At M=32, N=2, K=32 on a 16x4x16 array that is Bc=512, Br=32, d=128. The array is
// VersaCore's single spatial unrolling (Mu, Ku, Nu); datagen.py reads it from the cluster
// cfg, so retuning the array retunes the geometry here with no edit in this file.
//
// Bc and d are independent, so the two matmuls have different shapes. datagen.py derives
// shape 2 (O^T = V^T.P^T, M*meshRow = d, K*tileSize = Bc) from the same values and appends
// its S2_* descriptors to data.h; gemm_set_shape() switches between them per dispatch.
//
// M and N are swapped relative to the untransposed form because attention is computed
// TRANSPOSED here: S^T = (Q.K^T)^T = K.Q^T is the same GEMM with its operands swapped, so
// D = [M*meshRow, N*meshCol] = [Bc, Br]. That orientation is what lets StreamReduce's
// LANEWISE mode take the rowmax with no horizontal fold -- one lane is one query row, so
// the reduction runs along beats. See snax-flashattn.c.

{
  // gemm configurations
  // KV tiles the software pipeline streams. Keep it >= 4, so the pipeline has enough
  // tiles to hide the per-tile fill and drain, and a power of two, so the kernel's
  // l-recurrence invariant can check l == NKV * rowsum by its exponent alone.
  NKV: 4,

  K: 32,
  N: 2,
  M: 32,

  // C is read in FULL, not broadcast. The C port is serialised 4:1 onto 32 channels, and
  // a broadcast operand cannot be spread across a serialised input; the kernel needs the
  // full read anyway, because the second matmul accumulates O += P.V through C in place.
  broadcast_C: 0,
  channel_en_C: 1,
}
